Price a benchmark without launching anything. No compute. Requires benchmark access.

Two modes, exactly one per call:
- Before creating: pass the same arguments you will pass to createBenchmark (name, groupId,
  models, evaluators, metrics, ...). Returns {valid, weightedHours, jobs, notes} for the first
  launch, every cell, which is what createBenchmark launches at once with autoRun on. When the
  body has problems it returns valid=false with problems (codes and JSON pointers) and warnings
  instead, so this one call checks and prices. Show the price, then call createBenchmark with
  the same arguments.
- An existing benchmark: pass benchmarkId. Returns {weightedHours, jobs, cells} for its pending
  cells; all zero when nothing is pending. retry=true includes failed/stopped cells; rerun=true
  prices a fresh run of every cell, including completed work. Estimates are not spending caps.
  To price a change before making it, call
  validateBenchmark with the update body.

```ts
mcp__Tamarind_Bio__estimateBenchmark(args: {
// Benchmarkid
//
// An existing benchmark, to price its pending cells. Omit it and pass createBenchmark's arguments instead to price a benchmark before creating it.
benchmarkId?: string | null, // default: null
// Retry
//
// Include failed and stopped cells in an existing benchmark's estimate.
retry?: boolean, // default: false
// Rerun
//
// Price all cells as a fresh run, including completed cells. Existing benchmarks only; mutually exclusive with retry.
rerun?: boolean, // default: false
// Name
//
// Benchmark name, 1 to 200 characters.
name?: string | null, // default: null
// Groupid
//
// The MolDB group holding the dataset rows (one row per molecule). Make one with importMoleculeFile or createMolecules, or find one with listMoleculeGroups.
groupId?: string | null, // default: null
// Models
//
// Models, each {"key", "tool", "modelId"?, "label"?, "settings"?, "inputMap"?, "outputs"?, "rowSettings"?, "designs"?, "color"?, "run"?, "kind"?}. key matches [a-z0-9_-]{1,40} and is never reused. tool is a Tamarind tool ("mol-1") or a pinned custom tool ("user:<org>/<tool>@v<N>"). outputs maps slot -> the tool's output column. modelId names a finetuned model this entry SERVES: the id of a model trained by that custom tool (its version must declare a @tm.predictor; ids come from listFinetunedModels). The entry then runs the predictor with that model on every input, settings/inputMap are checked against the predictor's inputs and outputs map the predictor's columns; compare two of your models by giving each its own entry. Changing the model of an existing key is refused like changing its tool: add a new key. color ("#rrggbb") is the model's color on every chart; display only, it can change freely. run (a runId from the benchmark's runs; default "latest") pins the results shown for this model and its evaluators' scores of it to that run; it never changes what runs. kind (tool|design) overrides how the views read it (display only). tool "imported" is a model whose results already sit on the rows: as properties (e.g. a published predictor's scores, {"key": "mol1", "tool": "imported", "outputs": {"iptm": "row.properties.mol1__iptm"}}) or as scores an earlier tool run wrote ({"outputs": {"score": "row.scores.alphafold.ipTM"}}; "row.scores.alphafold@<jobId>.ipTM" reads that job's score instead of the latest run's); it runs nothing, costs nothing, takes no settings/inputMap, and no evaluator scores it. designs ({"group": "<MolDB group id>", "rowBy": "<row property>"}) on a "dataset" model imports designs made elsewhere: that group's molecules attach to rows by that property, and the evaluators score them as a design model's designs. Empty for a score-existing-molecules benchmark; models or no models is fixed at creation.
models?: { [key: string]: any }[] | null, // default: null
// Evaluators
//
// Evaluators, each {"key", "tool", "label"?, "settings"?, "inputMap"?, "appliesTo"? ("all" or [model keys]), "rowSettings"?}. rowSettings, keyed by row id or name, may set only what picks the target (chains, residues, sequences, ligand codes); every other setting belongs in settings. With models, each runs once per (row, model) and must bind `model`; without models, once per row.
evaluators?: { [key: string]: any }[] | null, // default: null
// Metrics
//
// Metrics, each {"key", "label"?, "source", "better"? (max|min), "aggregate"?, "designReduce"?, "ranked"?, "decimals"?, "tieMargin"?}. Display only, changeable freely: ranked (default true; false shows the values with no rank, star, colors or winners), decimals (0-10 places; default 3 significant figures), tieMargin (Compare models calls two values this close a tie). An evaluator or modelOutput source may add "chain": <n>|mean|min|max to read a per-chain score (columns nested under chain_<n>). A source is one of {"evaluator", "column"}, {"modelOutput"}, {"property"}, {"rowScore"}, {"designProperty"}, {"compare": {...}}, or {"allOf": [{"metric": key, "pass": ">=0.23"}, ...]}: 2 to 6 gates on other metrics, 1 when every gate passes on the same design or row, 0 when one fails, no value when one is missing; aggregate mean is then the share passing every gate (e.g. a protein-ligand success of RMSD "<2" and LDDT-PLI ">0.8"). A metric with ranked false shows no colors unless resultsConfig.colors gives it a heatmap direction.
metrics?: { [key: string]: any }[] | null, // default: null
// Subtasks
//
// Optional slices of the rows (up to 20), each scored by its own evaluators and metrics and shown as its own Results tab: {"key", "label"?, "rows", "evaluators": [evaluator keys], "metrics": [metric keys], "modelSettings"?: {modelKey: {setting: value}}, "order"?}. key matches [a-z0-9_-]{1,40} and a removed one is never reused. rows is {"property": rowProperty, "equals": value or [values]} (compared as text) or {"groupId": "<MolDB group id>"} (the rows also in that group); a row may be in several subtasks, and editing its property moves it. An evaluator named by one or more subtasks runs ONLY on their rows; one named by none runs on every row. A metric named by a subtask is computed over that subtask's rows on its tab; the overall (All) view pools a metric only across the subtasks that name that same key, so one key is one definition and pooling never mixes criteria: give each criterion its own metric key. A metric named by none follows the evaluators it reads when they all belong to subtasks, else applies to every row. modelSettings overrides a model's settings on the subtask's rows; order (0-1000) is the tab position. Example, FoldBench in one benchmark with a row property "task" of PP, AbAg or PL: [{"key": "pp", "label": "Protein-protein", "rows": {"property": "task", "equals": "PP"}, "evaluators": ["dockq"], "metrics": ["dockq_success"]}, {"key": "abag", "label": "Antibody-antigen", "rows": {"property": "task", "equals": "AbAg"}, "evaluators": ["dockq"], "metrics": ["dockq_success"]}, {"key": "pl", "label": "Protein-ligand", "rows": {"property": "task", "equals": "PL"}, "evaluators": ["lig"], "metrics": ["pl_success", "lig_rmsd", "lddt_pli"]}], where pl_success is an allOf metric ({"allOf": [{"metric": "lig_rmsd", "pass": "<2"}, {"metric": "lddt_pli", "pass": ">0.8"}]}); the All view pools dockq_success over PP + AbAg and never mixes it with pl_success. Omitted or [], the benchmark has no subtasks and behaves exactly as before.
subtasks?: { [key: string]: any }[] | null, // default: null
// Resultsconfig
//
// {"truth": [row property keys or "tool.column" scores used as truth], "hiddenProperties": [], "metricOrder": [], "hiddenModels": [], "panels": [custom chart tabs]}. A panel is {"title": "<tab name, unique>", "charts": [...]} (up to 6 panels, 8 charts each); a chart is {"type": "scatter", "x": metricKey, "y": metricKey, "fitLine"?: true, "title"?} (one point per input and model, with each model's Pearson r and Spearman rho) or {"type": "histogram", "metric": metricKey, "title"?} or {"type": "box"|"violin", "metric": metricKey, "groupBy"?: rowProperty, "level"?: "input"|"design", "title"?} (each model's spread of values; groupBy splits each model by a row property, one box per model and value) or {"type": "confusion", "metric": classificationMetricKey, "threshold"?, "title"?} (a 2x2 TP/FP/FN/TN grid per model for a compare against a class; a score is cut at the chart's threshold, else the metric's, else each model's best Youden cut). Charts plot metric keys, so add a metric for anything you want to chart. "colors": {metricKey: {"mode": "heatmap"|"cutoffs", "tiers": [...], "direction"?, "range"?}} colors the Results board per metric. heatmap (the default) shades each row from worst to best model by the metric's `better`; its optional range {"min"?, "max"?} (heatmap only; at least one end, min < max) fixes the scale instead of comparing the models within each row, e.g. DockQ {"min": 0, "max": 1} or RMSD {"min": 0, "max": 5}: values outside clamp to the ends, a missing end is the row's own lowest/highest value, and `better` (or direction) still decides which end is best; its optional direction follows a row column instead: {"byColumn": key, "positiveClass": "true"} or {"byColumn": key, "threshold": 10, "positiveIf": ">="|"<="} (exactly one of positiveClass/threshold; heatmap only): rows the column marks positive read the metric's way, the rest the reverse, rows without the column stay uncolored. cutoffs uses up to 6 tiers {"label", "min" or "max" (exactly one), "color": green|lightgreen|yellow|orange|red|blue|purple|gray}; the strictest tier a value reaches wins. Panels and colors are presentation only: they run nothing. Optional view settings (display only; unset keeps the default; naming a metric or model the benchmark lacks only warns): "leaderboard": {"sortBy"?: metric, "dir": best|worst, "star": rank1|none}; "board" (Results table): {"sort"?: {"key": name|avg:<metric>|measured:<metric>|m:<metric>:<model>, "dir": asc|desc}, "hiddenMetrics": [], "bestInRow": unique|ties|off}; "tabs": {"hidden": [leaderboard|results|distributions|predictions|worst|headToHead|methodology]}; "analysis": {"designPick": reduce|best|all} (a design model on an input: its designs combined by designReduce as the leaderboard, its best design, or every design); "hardest": {"metric"?, "model"?, "n" (0 = all), "across": model|mean|min}; "headToHead": {"metric"?, "a"?, "b"?}; "distributions": {"view": bars|curves, "bins", "sharedAxis"?}; "ranking": {"rows": shared|own|{"minCoverage": 0-1}, "includeRunning", "failed": skip|count} (which rows each metric is computed on: shared, the default, is the rows every ranked model has a value for; own, each model's own; minCoverage leaves models with values on fewer rows out of the shared rows, unranked; failed count keeps a row whose job failed or was stopped as one non-pass of a pass_rate metric); "intervals": {"level"?: 0.5-0.999 (default 0.95), "baseline"?: modelKey} (bootstrap confidence intervals on the leaderboard values and, with a baseline, each model's paired difference from it); "structures": true|false (the structure viewer; unset, structure prediction only).
resultsConfig?: { [key: string]: any } | null, // default: null
// Description
//
// Optional description, up to 4000 characters.
description?: string | null, // default: null
// Task
//
// A display hint only. Defaults to custom.
